INT. FLOODED CLINIC, SECOND-FLOOR WARD - NIGHT

Brown water laps at the stairwell below. Generators mutter. Camp beds line the corridor, each lit by a headlamp or a candle in a jar. The air smells of bleach and river.

DR. CATALINA OROZCO, 50s, surgical cap, sleeves rolled, kneels at a bedside with a stethoscope. NURSE EZEKIEL BWALYA, 30s, long hands, steady, hangs a bag of saline from a coat hook. And RAJ, 20s, an orderly with a flashlight, stands at the head of the stairs, watching the water.

DR. OROZCO
(to the ward)
Everyone who can sit up, please sit up. We are moving to the roof at midnight.

A murmur. Blankets rustle.

DR. OROZCO (CONT'D)
(to Ezekiel)
Pulse is one-twenty. Pressure?

EZEKIEL
Eighty over fifty. Dropping.

DR. OROZCO
(to herself)
Come on, little heart.

RAJ
(from the stairs)
Doctor, it's at the fourth step.

DR. OROZCO
Thank you, Raj. Keep counting.

EZEKIEL
(to the patient)
Sir, I'm going to give you something for the pain. Squeeze my hand.

DR. OROZCO ^
(to Ezekiel)
Double the dose.

EZEKIEL
(over her)
Double the dose?

DR. OROZCO
Yes. And his wife's too.

Ezekiel draws up a syringe. His hands never tremble. Orozco lays the stethoscope against the patient's chest and listens, eyes closed.

RAJ
(calling)
Fifth step!

DR. OROZCO
(to the ward)
No one panics. Stay in your beds. We will carry you.

She opens her eyes. The patient has stopped breathing. Ezekiel is already in motion. The headlamps shiver.
